# Chroma content pack Art and Writing: STEPS, the life events that give the pack's titles by the player's own act
# (moments 23 to 33 of MOMENTS-BRIEF.md, Part 4). Syntax: header of chroma-library/build.py. Rules: library-spec.md and
# the brief's pack rules. Names in title:, grants:, drops:, requires:, holds: and aims: come from art/catalogue.py,
# core/*.py and the base catalogue. Each life event is even on its own: alpha even, stakes 1.0, the five one-colour
# options plus the combination half named in its header comment (build.py D and PAIRSETS). per_year is the yearly rate
# of acting on the chance per eligible person (whoever holds a title or perk in holds:, inside the gates of
# engine-conditions.py LIFE_ART), estimates unless a source is named. Steps carry their real chances (the brief's pack
# rules), the same for every colour that tries for the same step inside a moment; the earned-odds lifts (helps.py,
# half on the summits) come on top in the engine. Steps at real odds are not long shots, so no option here carries
# grants_if_fails. The summits are facets: [renowned artist] on the visual career held, [celebrated author] on
# [novelist]. Every moment carries earth:, tribal: and magic: lines in the forms of art/worlds.py, and an untagged scene
# for each of the two other worlds; an option whose act reads differently there has its own wording. The combinations
# are the honest other ways through the moment: weighing the step, a smaller step, or turning it down. The wrongs
# come three to each colour (seven pairs and one single), marked and closed with a real backfire. Children appear only in supervised forms (a school
# class with the school's leave, parents' forms signed). No real person, gallery, museum, prize, publisher, school,
# brand, country or year is named. per_year is the brief's, except that a figure for a stage the moment does not list
# is set to 0 (coordinator, 10-10: the young_adult figure of 29, 30, 32 and 33; by the same rule the elder figure of 25).

# ================================================================ steps

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 23. art school applications (pairs D1a)
# Every colour applies at the real chance for an art school place (40 to 55): 45. Blue's way pays for a portfolio
# course (requires savings; someone without it borrows the fee: lacking .5). The pairs are the honest other roads: a
# year's work in the sign-writer's shop to save the fees, prints sold at the market instead, the free foundation year
# at the local college, the parents asked straight, and the school's art teacher asked to go through the portfolio.

== art school applications
tier: life event
stages: juvenile young_adult adult
age: 16-35
holds: drawing and painting | life-drawing regular
per_year: 0 .15 .08 .02 0 0
drivers: prosper+.2 money+.2
times: per person aged 16 to 35 who draws (the base skill or the life class) a year: about 1 in 7 at seventeen or eighteen, and fewer later, send a portfolio to an art school; creative arts and design is about 1 university student in 12 in the UK, and about 15 lives in 1,000 study fine art, illustration or design (UK higher-education statistics; catalogue share; estimate)
likelier: an art teacher who pushes the portfolio, a foundation year done, good years with grants and loans easy to get, parents who back it
rarer: no portfolio, fees the family cannot meet, parents who want a safer subject, a life already full of work and children
gap: 1-3
domain: career.5
rite: young_adult
tone: mixed
life: school, work
horizon: months
roles: mentor, parent, grandparent, friend, rival
earth: the art school applications: a form, a statement and a portfolio for the interview
tribal: the painter of the great cave will take an apprentice for three winters, and the young ones bring their marks
magic: the academy of arts takes new students once a year, and each one brings a folio to the masters
scene: The applications close after the holidays: a form, a statement, and a portfolio of twenty pieces for an interview in the spring. {Ns} drawings cover the bedroom floor, and {mentor} has said more than once that {N} should apply.
scene W: {N} has read the guidance three times, and every piece is mounted, labelled and in the order the course asks for.
scene U: {N} keeps thinking about what the interviewers really look for: not finished pictures, but the thinking, the sketchbooks, the mistakes worked through.
scene B: Three art schools matter and the rest are back-ups. {N} wants the one whose graduates end up on the walls of the big galleries.
scene R: {N} cannot imagine spending a life on anything else. The portfolio is a mess of everything at once, and every page of it is alive.
scene G: The drawings are of home: the allotments, the old men on the bench by the bus stop, the hands of {grandparent}. {N} wonders whether art school is a way out, or a way further in.
scene tribal: The painter of the great cave will take one apprentice this winter, for three winters. The young ones of the band lay their marks out by the fire, scratched on bark and bone and smoothed hide.
scene magic: The academy of arts opens its gates to new students once a year. Each one brings a folio for the masters, and the fees are paid in silver at the porter's lodge.
- W | send the portfolio exactly as the guidance asks, every piece mounted and labelled | title: art school student | v: conformity | tribal: lay out the marks exactly as the painter asked, each in its place by the fire | magic: send the folio exactly as the academy's rules ask, every leaf mounted and labelled | foot: place learn.higher | chance: 45
- U | take a paid portfolio course on Saturdays, and apply with what it teaches | title: art school student | requires: savings | without: means | lacking: .5 | habit | self_control: + | v: achievement, conformity | tribal: give a good fur to sit with the painter's helper for a moon, and bring what you learn | magic: pay a master for a season of lessons, and apply with what they teach | foot: person teacher | chance: 45
- B | apply only to the school whose graduates end up in the big galleries | title: art school student | aims: painter or sculptor | v: achievement, power | tribal: ask only the painter whose apprentices are wanted at every band's cave | magic: apply only to the academy whose students end up painting for the court | foot: place learn.higher | chance: 45
- R | throw out the safe pieces, and fill the portfolio with the wild new work | title: art school student | mark: took a wild risk | v: stimulation | tribal: throw away the careful marks, and bring the wild ones made in the night | magic: throw out the careful studies, and fill the folio with the wild new work | chance: 45
- G | apply to the art school in your own town, with home in every drawing | title: art school student | mark: stayed home | v: tradition, benevolence | tribal: ask the painter of your own band's cave, with your own hills in every mark | magic: apply to the academy in your own city, with its streets in every leaf | foot: place learn.higher | chance: 45
- BG | work a year in the sign-writer's shop in town, and save the fees | grants: savings | self_control: + | v: power, tradition | tribal: scrape hides for the elders through a winter, and save the gifts the painter asks | magic: work a year in the sign-painter's shop, and save the academy's fees in silver | chance: 75
- BR | skip art school, and sell prints at the weekend market instead | grants: selling | mark: turned down a chance | v: self-direction, stimulation | tribal: skip the painter's cave, and trade your marked hides at the gathering instead | magic: skip the academy, and sell prints at the market cross instead | foot: place comm.market | chance: 75
- UG | take the free foundation year at the local college before choosing | grants: sculpting | door | v: achievement, security | tribal: spend a winter learning to carve from the old carver of your band before choosing | magic: take the free year at the town's drawing school before choosing | foot: place learn.higher | chance: 75
- WR | tell your parents straight that this is the life you want, and ask their blessing | v: benevolence, conformity | tribal: tell your mother and father at the fire that this is the life you want, and ask their blessing | foot: means freedom+.03 | chance: 75
- WU | ask the art teacher at school to go through the portfolio piece by piece | grants: mentor | door | v: achievement, conformity | tribal: ask the old woman who paints the shields to judge every mark, one by one | magic: ask your old drawing master to go through the folio leaf by leaf | chance: 75
works: A letter comes in the spring with the college's crest on it, and in the autumn {N} carries a portfolio case up the stairs of the art school.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the drawing goes on, and a year later {N} is glad of the road taken.
fails: The letter is short and kind: many strong applicants this year, and no place.
fails: The interview goes badly, a question about a favourite painter leaves {N} blank, and the train home is very long.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 24. the degree show (pairs D1b)
# The crossing the three-year status waits for (tenure 2 or more): each colour takes a different road out, at that
# road's real chance, as the brief gives them. White trains to teach (55; teacher training wants a degree: requires
# graduate, without it only by the college's leave, lacking .3), Blue answers an art director's brief (25), Black works
# the private view for a dealer (20), Red paints the college a mural (30), Green keeps the year group together in a
# shared studio (56, others and the rent have to say yes). The roads are uneven, so the pairs even the colours out: a
# steady framing job (86) and the show priced and sold (86) for the colours whose roads are hardest, an assistant's
# year with a sculptor (78), a summer at home painting (82), and the home town's one residency (25), a real long try
# for White and Green, whose roads are easiest. Each colour's mean comes within about two points.

== the degree show
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult
age: 19-45
holds: art school student
tenure: 2-100
per_year: 0 0 .6 .6 0 0
drivers: prosper+.3
times: per art school student in the last year of the course: once, at the degree show; most graduates of fine art and illustration work outside the arts within a few years, and about 1 in 10 is earning from art five years on; about 15 lives in 1,000 hold an art school place (catalogue share; graduate outcome surveys; estimate)
likelier: a final year that went well, a show in a city with galleries and agencies, good years for galleries and publishers
rarer: a course left early, a show far from where art is bought, a lean year
gap: 2-4
domain: career.5
rite: young_adult
tone: mixed
life: work, school
horizon: months
roles: mentor, friend, parent, colleague, rival
earth: the degree show: three years of work on the walls for four days, and the city comes to look
tribal: the apprentice's three winters end, and the bands come to see the first wall painted alone
magic: the academy's graduation hall, where every student hangs a year's work for the city and the guilds
scene: The degree show opens on Thursday night: {Ns} space at the end of the corridor, three years of work on the walls, a price list on the table and a bowl of crisps going soft. Dealers, art directors, teachers, parents and a reporter from the local paper drift through the noise.
scene W: {N} has hung the space to the centimetre and printed a list of every piece with its medium and its size. The show is a promise kept to three years of tutors.
scene U: {N} watches who stops and for how long, and which piece holds them. By nine o'clock {N} has a theory about it.
scene B: {N} can tell which visitors are dealers by their shoes, and has a card ready for each of them.
scene R: Three years of work, and tonight the walls are finally full. {N} wants to shout, and settles for dancing in the corridor after the speeches.
scene G: {Ns} family have come on the coach and stand before every piece as if in church, and the year group are already talking about what happens next, and whether it can happen together.
scene tribal: The apprentice's three winters are ending. At the gathering the bands crowd into the side cave to see the first wall {N} has painted alone, and the traders in carvings look as carefully as the elders.
scene magic: The academy's great hall is hung with every student's year of work, and the city comes to look: guild masters, picture dealers from the square, drawing masters from the town schools, and proud parents in their best.
- W | sign up for a year's teacher training, to teach art in a school | title: art teacher | binds | v: benevolence, conformity | tribal: ask to sit beside the one who teaches the children the marks, and learn to teach them | magic: ask a drawing master to take you on as an under-master for a year | chance: 55
- U | send the art director who lingered at the show a drawing made to her brief | title: illustrator for hire | aims: illustrator for hire | v: achievement, conformity | tribal: mark a bowl in the trader's own pattern, and send it to him the next morning | magic: send the printer who lingered at the wall a page illuminated to his own brief | chance: 25
- B | work the private view until the dealers know the name and the prices | grants: a dealer who sells your work | v: power, achievement | tribal: walk with the traders all evening, until they know the name and what each piece costs | magic: work the hall all evening, until the picture dealers know the name and the prices | chance: 20
- R | offer the college a mural for the wall by its gates, painted in a weekend | title: street artist | body: light | v: stimulation | tribal: offer to paint the wall at the cave mouth, and finish it before the gathering ends | magic: offer the academy a mural for the wall by its gate, and paint it in a weekend | chance: 30
- G | keep the year together in a shared studio at the old mill, rent split six ways | grants: a circle of artists | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: keep the apprentices together, painting side by side in the old cave by the river | magic: keep the year together in a shared studio above the dyers' yard, rent split six ways | chance: 56
- RG | go home for the summer, and paint whatever comes, with no plan at all | mark: came home | v: hedonism, tradition | tribal: go back to your own band for the summer, and paint whatever comes | magic: go home to your village for the summer, and paint whatever comes | chance: 82
- UB | price every piece sensibly, and sell what sells to the show's visitors | grants: selling | v: achievement, power | tribal: set a fair price on every piece, and trade what the visitors will take | magic: price every piece sensibly, and sell what sells to the hall's visitors | foot: means money+.06 | chance: 86
- UR | go and assist a working sculptor in the next city, mixing plaster and learning | grants: sculpting | door | binds | v: achievement, stimulation | tribal: go and help the old carver of the next band, grinding stone and learning | magic: go and assist a guild sculptor in the next city, mixing plaster and learning | foot: person mentor | chance: 78
- WB | take a steady job in a framing shop, and keep a foot in the trade | grants: contact in the trade | v: security, achievement | tribal: take a steady place stretching hides for the traders, and keep near the trade | magic: take a steady place in a frame-maker's shop, and keep a foot in the trade | chance: 86
- WG | go home, and apply for the arts centre's one residency, teaching its Saturday classes | grants: studio of one's own | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: go back to your own band, and ask the elders for the bare wall of the old shelter | magic: go home, and ask the guildhall for the one painting room it gives a young painter | chance: 25
works: A week after the show {N} has an answer, an address and a start date, and the summer has a shape.
works: By the last day the visitors' book is full, and the road {N} chose opens the way it should.
fails: The show closes on Sunday, the work comes down into a borrowed van, and the phone stays quiet.
fails: The one visitor who mattered never comes back, and {N} goes home to a bedroom stacked with canvases.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 25. a wall that needs a mural (pairs D2a)
# The council's call for a mural: every colour's design wins at the real chance for a mural commission (30 to 40): 35,
# and the first paid wall makes a [street artist]. Green's way is the town's own wall painted with a school class: the
# art teacher's road ([a mural the town loves], not the career); anyone else leads a class only by the school's leave
# (requires art teacher, without: approval, lacking .3), and the children paint in school hours with the parents'
# forms signed. The pairs: the wall painted for nothing, the canal walls instead, a hand lent to the painter who wins,
# the old library mural restored, and a wrong in White and Green: the residents' committee turned against the outsider
# the council picked. per_year keeps the brief's values with the stray elder figure set to 0, as the stages are written.

== a wall that needs a mural
tier: life event
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature
age: 16-80
holds: life-drawing regular | art school student | art teacher
per_year: 0 .02 .1 .08 .04 0
drivers: community+.3 prosper+.1
times: per life-drawing regular, art student or art teacher a year: about 1 in 10 young adults and fewer later answer a call for a mural on a public wall; councils, schools and shops pay for murals in most towns, and about 1 life in 2,000 is paid to paint walls for a year or more (catalogue share; estimate)
likelier: a council with money for its streets, a town that likes its walls painted, a portfolio of walls already done, friends on the residents' committee
rarer: a council cutting back, a town that wants its walls plain, no wall work to show
gap: 2-5
domain: career.3 community.3
tone: joy
life: work, community
horizon: months
roles: friend, elder, rival, boss, colleague
earth: the council wants a mural for the long wall of the underpass, and asks local painters for designs
tribal: the band's new winter shelter has a long bare wall, and the elders want it painted before the snows
magic: the quarter's council wants the blank wall of the old granary painted, and asks the city's painters for designs
scene: The council has pinned a notice in the library: the long grey wall of the underpass by the station is to have a mural, designs by the end of the month, a small fee, the paint and the scaffold provided. {friend} has photographed the notice and sent it to {N} twice.
scene W: A wall the whole town walks past every day deserves a design agreed with the people who live beside it, and paint that will last twenty years.
scene U: {N} has paced the wall out and noticed the drainpipes, the cracks and the way the light falls at four o'clock. Every one of them could be part of the picture.
scene B: The underpass is on the way to the station: thousands of people a day. {N} thinks about whose name would be on that wall, and what it would bring.
scene R: {N} can already see it: colour from end to end, something huge and alive where the grey has been for forty years.
scene G: {N} grew up on this estate, and so did {Ns} parents. The wall should look like the people who walk past it.
scene tribal: The band has dug its new winter shelter into the hillside, and its long back wall is bare rock. The elders want it painted before the first snows, and they ask who will do it.
scene magic: The quarter's council wants the blank wall of the old granary on the square painted, and has nailed a notice to the well: designs to the clerk by the new moon, a small purse, and the paint paid.
- W | plan the design with the neighbours and the council, squared up to last | title: street artist | v: conformity, benevolence | tribal: plan the wall with the elders and the families of the shelter, every mark agreed | magic: plan the design with the neighbours and the quarter's council, squared up to last | chance: 35
- U | design it around the wall's cracks and drainpipes, so the picture seems to belong there | title: street artist | v: achievement, conformity | tribal: draw the design around the rock's own cracks and bulges, so the animals seem to move | magic: design it around the granary's cracks and beams, so the picture seems to belong there | chance: 35
- B | get the local paper to print your design before the council votes | title: street artist | v: power, achievement | tribal: show your design to every family before the elders choose, until the band talks of nothing else | magic: get the broadsheet to print your design before the council votes | chance: 35
- R | send in a design so bold the committee cannot look away from it | title: street artist | aims: painter or sculptor | mark: took a wild risk | v: stimulation | tribal: show the elders a design so bold they cannot look away from it | magic: send in a design so bold the council cannot look away from it | chance: 35
- G | paint it with a class from the school, the parents' forms signed | grants: a mural the town loves | requires: art teacher | without: approval | lacking: .3 | v: universalism, benevolence | tribal: paint it with the children of the band, their mothers sitting by | magic: paint it with a class from the quarter's school, their master alongside | chance: 35
- BG | paint it for nothing, for the name it will bring in town | grants: name on the local scene | v: achievement, universalism | tribal: paint it for no gift, for the name it will bring among the families | magic: paint it for no purse, for the name it will bring in the quarter | chance: 74
- BR | turn it down, and paint the empty garage walls by the canal on your own terms | grants: following online | mark: turned down a chance | v: self-direction, stimulation | tribal: turn it down, and paint the rocks by the river where nobody tells you what to paint | magic: turn it down, and paint the empty warehouse walls by the canal on your own terms | chance: 74
- UR | help the painter who wins, and learn how a wall that size is done | grants: a circle of artists | mark: made a friend | binds | v: achievement, stimulation | tribal: help the painter the elders choose, and learn how a wall that size is done | chance: 74
- WG | get the residents' committee to reject the outsider the council picked | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: shutting out the council's chosen painter; backfire: the council goes ahead anyway, and the painter it chose tells the local paper who blocked her | v: tradition, conformity | tribal: get the families of the shelter to refuse the painter the elders brought from another band | magic: get the quarter's guild to refuse the painter the council brought from across the river | chance: 72
- WU | offer to restore the faded old mural on the library wall instead | grants: restoring old things | v: conformity, security | tribal: offer to renew the faded marks in the old shelter instead | magic: offer to restore the faded old mural on the chapter house wall instead | foot: place learn.keeping | chance: 76
works: The council chooses {Ns} design in the spring, and by early summer the underpass is scaffolded and {N} is up a ladder in old clothes.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the wall gets painted, and people stop on the way to the station to look at it.
fails: The council chooses a design from a firm in the city, and thanks every local painter in a printed letter.
fails: The money is cut in the autumn, and the wall stays grey for another year.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 26. the first commission (pairs D2b)
# The first paid brief: every colour that takes it on becomes an [illustrator for hire] at the real chance for a first
# illustration commission (35 to 45): 40. The gate (LIFE_ART) keeps it to someone not yet an illustrator with time on
# the rung below. The pairs: the fee waived for the path-keepers' cause, a single drawing first to learn the trade, the
# contract read before signing, the job passed to a friend who needs it, and a wrong in Blue and Black: photographs
# traced and sent as drawings.

== the first commission
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-90
holds: art school student | life-drawing regular | street artist
per_year: 0 0 .1 .08 .04 .02
drivers: era+.2 prosper+.2
times: per art student, life-drawing regular or street artist a year: about 1 in 10 young adults and fewer later are offered a first paid drawing job (a cover, a label, a set of drawings for a book); about 3 lives in 1,000 work as freelance illustrators for a year or more (catalogue share; estimate)
likelier: drawings shown online or at a show, a friend who works in publishing or design, a boom in illustrated books and packaging
rarer: no portfolio anyone can see, a lean year for small presses, a life far from where books and magazines are made
gap: 2-4
domain: career.5
tone: joy
life: work
horizon: months
roles: boss, friend, colleague, mentor
earth: a small press wants a cover and twelve drawings for a book, paid, in three weeks
tribal: a trader from the next band wants his drums and bowls marked with a pattern of his own
magic: a printer on the bridge wants a frontispiece and a dozen cuts for an almanac, paid in silver
scene: An email from a small press: they have seen {Ns} drawings online and want a cover and twelve drawings for a book of country walks, sold to raise money for keeping the paths open. The fee is small, the deadline is three weeks, and they need an answer by Friday.
scene W: {N} reads the brief twice and writes down every drawing it asks for, with a date beside each one.
scene U: The brief is vague in all the interesting places. {N} starts sketching three different books in the margin of the email.
scene B: A cover with {Ns} name on it, in shops. {N} wonders what the press really pays, and what it would pay if asked.
scene R: Someone wants to pay for drawings! {N} rings {friend} and shouts down the phone before reading to the end.
scene G: The walks are the ones {N} grew up on: the river path, the beech wood, the old drove road. {N} knows every stile.
scene tribal: A trader from the next band has seen the marks on {Ns} shield and wants his own drums and bowls marked with a pattern nobody else carries. He has brought good flint to pay.
scene magic: A printer from the bridge has seen {Ns} sketches in the tavern and wants a frontispiece and a dozen cuts for next year's almanac, paid in silver, the blocks by the new moon.
- W | deliver every drawing to the brief, on the day it was promised | title: illustrator for hire | mark: kept your word | self_control: + | v: conformity | tribal: mark every drum and bowl to the trader's pattern, on the day you promised | magic: deliver every cut to the printer's brief, on the day it was promised | chance: 40
- U | send three roughs that answer the brief three different ways | title: illustrator for hire | v: achievement, conformity | tribal: show the trader three patterns that answer his wish three different ways | magic: send three trial sketches that answer the brief three different ways | chance: 40
- B | quote a proper rate, and ask for the name on the cover | title: illustrator for hire | v: power, achievement | tribal: ask a proper price in flint, and for your own sign beside his on every drum | magic: name a proper price, and ask for the name on the title page | chance: 40
- R | stay up three nights, and draw the whole book in one burst | title: illustrator for hire | body: light | v: stimulation, hedonism | tribal: sit up three nights by the fire, and mark every drum and bowl in one burst | magic: sit up three nights by the candle, and cut every block in one burst | chance: 40
- G | walk every route in the book, and draw each path from life | title: illustrator for hire | v: tradition, universalism | tribal: walk to the trader's own camp, and mark his things with the animals of his hills | magic: walk every road in the almanac, and draw each one from life | chance: 40
- RG | waive the fee, since the book's money goes to keeping the paths open | mark: helped someone in need | v: benevolence, stimulation | tribal: mark the drums for no flint, since they are for the gathering's dances | magic: waive the silver, since the almanac's money goes to mending the roads | chance: 76
- UB | trace the reference photographs, and send them as drawings | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: traced photographs passed off as drawings; backfire: the photographer finds the book in a shop, and the press pays nothing and says why | self_control: - | v: achievement, power | tribal: press the trader's old bowl into wet clay, and pass the copy off as your own marking | magic: trace the old engravings in the printer's own cupboard, and send them as new cuts | chance: 74
- UG | ask for a single drawing first, to learn how a brief is done | grants: illustration | v: achievement, security | tribal: ask to mark a single bowl first, to learn what a trader wants | magic: ask for a single cut first, to learn how a printer's brief is done | foot: person boss | chance: 76
- WB | have the contract read line by line before signing anything | grants: striking a deal | v: security, power | tribal: have the elders hear the bargain spoken out before you shake on it | magic: have a notary read the contract line by line before you sign | foot: lesson know the bargain before agreeing | chance: 76
- WR | pass the job to a friend from the class who needs the money more | mark: helped someone in need | v: benevolence, stimulation | tribal: send the trader to a friend who paints shields and needs the flint more | magic: pass the job to a friend from the academy who needs the silver more | chance: 76
works: The press loves the cover, the book comes back from the printer with {Ns} name on the back flap, and a second email arrives from a firm that saw it.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the three weeks go well, and the book of walks is in the shops by the spring.
fails: The press goes with an illustrator they used before, and sends a polite email about next time.
fails: The deadline slips, the drawings arrive a week late, and the press uses photographs instead.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 27. the first show (pairs D3a)
# A first show of one's own that sells makes a [painter or sculptor] at the real chance (20 to 30): 25 in every colour,
# with [a name in the art world] for four of them and, for Black's collectors and critics, [a dealer who sells your
# work]. The gate (LIFE_ART) asks for years on the rung below (tenure 2 or more). The pairs: the café show on the high
# street, the opening turned into a party (giving in: the drink), the gallery's past sales asked for before signing, the
# show turned down until the work is ready, and a talk given in the gallery.

== the first show
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 19-95
holds: life-drawing regular | street artist | illustrator for hire | art school student
tenure: 2-100
per_year: 0 0 .06 .06 .04 .02
drivers: prosper+.3 fortune+.2
times: per person two years or more on the rung below (the life class, walls, illustration or art school) a year: about 1 in 16 are offered a first show of their own in a gallery, a café or a library; most first shows sell little, and about 1 life in 1,000 earns a real part of a living from painting or sculpture for some years (catalogue share; estimate)
likelier: good years when people buy art, a body of work already made, a friend who runs a gallery, a lucky cancellation
rarer: a lean year, no work finished, a town with no gallery and no café that hangs pictures
gap: 2-5
domain: career.5
tone: joy
life: work
horizon: months
roles: boss, friend, partner, rival, colleague
earth: a small gallery offers a first show of one's own: three weeks on its walls, the opening on a Friday
tribal: the traders in carvings will lay out one maker's pieces alone at the gathering, for every band to see
magic: a picture dealer offers a month in the small room of his hall on the square
scene: The small gallery off the high street has had a cancellation: three weeks in the spring, and the walls are {Ns} if {N} can fill them. The owner takes forty in every hundred of each sale, and wants the list of works in a month.
scene W: {N} makes a list: twenty pieces framed alike, a price on each that a teacher could afford, and a catalogue with every title spelled right.
scene U: {N} lays all the work out on the studio floor and looks for the thread that runs through it. A show is an argument, and this one is nearly there.
scene B: The gallery's mailing list has three collectors and a critic on it. {N} intends to meet all four before the wine runs out.
scene R: Two months is no time, and {N} does not care: there is a whole new body of work trying to get out, and the walls are waiting for it.
scene G: The pieces are of the town: the harbour wall, the allotments in winter, the faces of the neighbours who sat for them in their own kitchens.
scene tribal: At the gathering the traders in carvings will set out one maker's pieces alone on a hide by the great fire, and every band will walk past them. This year they have asked for {Ns}.
scene magic: A picture dealer on the square offers {N} a month in the small room of his hall, the city's collectors invited to the first evening, and a third of every sale for the house.
