scene: The secondary school's art teacher is retiring at the end of the spring term, and the post is in the local paper: art and design, full time, from the autumn. {colleague} says the art room is the best room in the building, and the head is desperate.
scene W: A school needs its art taught properly, every year group, every week, as carefully as anything else on the timetable. {N} could do that, and do it well.
scene U: {N} has always thought drawing can be taught like a language: line, tone, proportion, step by step, until any child can do it.
scene B: Head of art, in time. A budget, a department, and the school's walls to fill. {N} reads the pay scale twice.
scene R: {N} remembers {Ns} own art teacher, the one who let the class paint the ceiling. Every child deserves one of those.
scene G: It is the school down the road, where half the street went. The art room looks out over the playing fields and the hill behind.
scene tribal: The old woman who taught the children the marks and the patterns can no longer see the rock clearly. The elders look round the fire for someone who knows the marks and has patience for small hands.
scene magic: The town school needs a drawing master by the autumn, and the governors have posted the place on the guildhall door: a room, a salary, and forty children who have never held a silverpoint.
