scene: The old shoe shop on the corner has been empty for a year, its window full of dust and post. The landlord has put a card in it: a low rent for the first year, to the right tenant. {partner} walks past it every day, and has started to say "imagine".
scene W: A gallery would be a place the town could trust: fair prices, honest books, every artist paid on time. {N} has been thinking for years about how one ought to be run.
scene U: {N} has walked through the empty shop in the mind a dozen times: the light from the big window, the back room for framing, the long wall that would take the big canvases.
scene B: The corner is the best in town: the bus stop, the school run, the market on Saturdays. {N} knows exactly which painters would sell from that window.
scene R: {N} wants to get the door open and paint the inside of the window tonight. Wine on Fridays, music, people spilling out onto the pavement.
scene G: The shoe shop was there for sixty years. {N} would want the town's own makers in it: the potter, the weaver, the man who carves spoons on the allotments.
scene tribal: The old trader in carvings has died, and his place by the great fire at the gathering, where the far bands come to look and trade, stands empty. The elders ask who will keep it.
scene magic: The old picture dealer on the square has died, and his hall stands empty with its shutters down. The guild will let it to whoever can pay the first year's rent in silver on the quarter day.
