scene: An email from an editor at a big publisher: she read the first pages from the pile and wants the whole manuscript by the end of the month, if it is ready. {N} reads the email in the kitchen four times, and then rings {friend}.
scene W: {N} owes this book to the people in it, the ones whose story nobody else has told. It has to go out finished, and right.
scene U: {N} knows the middle sags. There is a month to fix it, and the whole thing is already pulled apart across the floor.
scene B: An editor at a big house is a door that does not open twice. {N} wants to know who else is on her list, and what they were paid.
scene R: Someone wants to read it, all of it! {N} has carried this book around like a fever for years.
scene G: The book is about the town and the family: the farm, the chapel, the long winters, the people who are gone. {N} made it so they would not be forgotten.
scene tribal: At the gathering the elders will hear one new long telling at the great fire, and if they nod, every band will learn it and carry it home. They have asked to hear {Ns}.
scene magic: A printer of the city has read the first pages and wants the whole manuscript by the next quarter day. If his reader likes it, it goes to the press, and the book is cried in the market.
