- UB | trade a read of the manuscript for editing work with a friend in publishing | grants: editing | v: achievement, power | tribal: trade a winter of hunting for a hearing from an old teller who knows the elders | magic: trade a read of the manuscript for copying work with a friend at the printer's | foot: person go-between | chance: 76
- UR | claim in the covering letter that a famous writer has read and loved it | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: a famous name claimed in a covering letter; backfire: the editor knows the writer, and asks | v: achievement, stimulation | tribal: tell the elders a famous teller of the old days gave you the telling | magic: claim in the letter that a famous poet has read and loved it | chance: 74
- WG | read the manuscript chapter by chapter to the writing group at the library | grants: a circle of artists | door | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: tell the telling night by night to the others at your own fire | magic: read the manuscript chapter by chapter to the writers' circle at the inn | chance: 76
- WR | send it out, and promise yourself to write every morning whatever comes back | grants: writing stories | habit | self_control: + | v: conformity, stimulation | tribal: tell it at the gathering, and promise yourself a new verse every morning whatever comes | foot: door kept your word | chance: 76
works: The editor rings on a Tuesday, a contract follows, and a year later there is a box of books on the doorstep with {Ns} name on every spine.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the manuscript goes out into the world, and the writing goes on.
fails: The editor writes back after six weeks: she loved the voice, but the list is full, and she wishes {N} luck.
fails: Nothing comes back at all, and the manuscript goes into a drawer with the others.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 32. a work in the national collection (pairs D5b)
# The visual summit, a facet on the career held (holds: names the four careers it sits on): a work bought for the
# national collection makes a [renowned artist] at the real chance (6 to 10): 8 in every colour. It is a step at real
# odds, not a long shot, so no perk is given on failure. The gate (LIFE_ART) asks for five years in the career
# (tenure 5). The pairs: the piece given to the
# town's own museum, lent for a year first, sold to the higher private bidder, the work of a lifetime catalogued, and a
# wrong in Black and Red: a buyer invented to hurry the curator.

== a work in the national collection
tier: life event
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-100
holds: painter or sculptor | street artist | illustrator for hire | master craftsperson
tenure: 5-100
per_year: 0 0 0 .02 .03 .02
drivers: era+.2 fortune+.3
times: per painter, sculptor, street artist, illustrator or master craftsperson of five years or more a year: about 1 in 40 in mid-life have a piece looked at for a national or regional public collection; national collections buy a handful of works by living artists each year, and about 1 life in 10,000 becomes a renowned artist (catalogue share; estimate)
likelier: a name in the art world, a dealer who shows the work in the capital, a body of work that has been seen, a fashion that turns toward the work
rarer: a career far from the capital, a year when the collection's money is cut, work nobody has seen
gap: 3-8
domain: career.5
tone: joy
life: work, meaning
horizon: months
roles: boss, mentor, partner, rival, friend
earth: a curator from the national gallery in the capital asks about a piece for the national collection
tribal: the elders of every band will choose one carving or painted hide to keep in the great cave for always
magic: the keeper of the realm's pictures asks about a piece for the royal gallery
scene: A curator from the national gallery in the capital stood for a long time before {Ns} piece in a mixed show, and has written to ask whether it might be offered to the national collection. The acquisitions committee meets in the spring, and buys perhaps one work in a hundred it is shown.
scene W: {N} thinks of the piece in the national collection, seen by school parties for a hundred years. It should go there properly, or not at all.
scene U: {N} has always known which piece holds the whole of the work in it. The question is whether the committee can be made to see it.
scene B: The national collection is the one line in a biography that changes every price after it. {N} rings the dealer before replying.
scene R: {N} reads the letter standing up in the cold studio, and has to sit down on a paint tin.
scene G: The piece is of the valley where {N} grew up, made over three winters. {N} wonders whether it belongs in the capital at all.
scene tribal: Once in a generation the elders of every band choose one carving or one painted hide to be carried to the great cave and kept there for always, where every band will see it. This winter they have come to look at {Ns}.
scene magic: The keeper of the realm's pictures has seen {Ns} work in the guildhall and asks whether a piece might be offered to the royal gallery. The court decides at midsummer, and it takes very few.
- W | offer the piece at a fair price, with its full history and condition report | title: renowned artist | aims: renowned artist | v: conformity | tribal: offer the piece freely, with its whole story told to the elders, from the first cut | magic: offer the piece at a fair price, with its full history and a report of its state | chance: 8
- U | write the curator an account of where the piece sits in a lifetime's work | title: renowned artist | v: achievement, conformity | tribal: tell the elders where the piece sits among all the work of your life | magic: write the keeper an account of where the piece sits in a lifetime's work | chance: 8
- B | let the dealer set a high price, and wait for the curator to come back | title: renowned artist | self_control: + | v: power, achievement | tribal: let the trader who carries your carvings ask a high gift, and wait for the elders to come back | magic: let the dealer set a high price, and wait for the keeper to come back | chance: 8
- R | offer the piece the curator did not ask for, the one that feels truer | title: renowned artist | mark: took a wild risk | v: stimulation, self-direction | tribal: offer the elders the piece they did not ask for, the one that feels truer | magic: offer the keeper the piece he did not ask for, the one that feels truer | chance: 8
- G | offer the piece made from the place and its people, and let it speak for them | title: renowned artist | v: tradition, universalism | tribal: offer the piece carved from the river stone of your own hills, and let it speak for them | chance: 8
- BR | tell the curator a private buyer wants the piece by Friday, though none does | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: a bluff to a public collection; backfire: the curator calls the bluff, and the committee buys another artist's work | v: power, stimulation | tribal: tell the elders a far band wants the piece by the next moon, though none does | magic: tell the keeper a duke wants the piece by the quarter day, though none does | chance: 74
- RG | give the piece to the town's own small museum instead, where the work began | grants: good name in town | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: give the piece to your own band's cave instead, where the work began | magic: give the piece to your own town's guildhall instead, where the work began | chance: 76
- UG | lend the piece for a year, so the curators can live with it before deciding | v: security, achievement | tribal: let the piece stay in the great cave for a winter, so the elders can live with it before choosing | magic: lend the piece for a year, so the court can live with it before deciding | foot: person keeper | chance: 76
- WB | sell it to the private collector who offered more, and tell the curator plainly | grants: savings | self_control: - | v: power, security | tribal: trade it to the far band that offered more, and tell the elders plainly | magic: sell it to the merchant who offered more, and tell the keeper plainly | chance: 76
- WU | catalogue the work of a lifetime properly, every piece dated and photographed | grants: a body of work | habit | v: security, achievement | tribal: lay out the work of a lifetime in the cave, every piece in the order it was made | magic: catalogue the work of a lifetime properly, every piece dated and drawn in a book | chance: 76
works: The committee meets, and the letter that follows carries the national gallery's crest at the top and a date for the hanging.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the piece goes where {N} meant it to go, and the work goes on.
fails: The committee admires the piece and buys another, and the curator's letter is warm and short.
fails: The collection's money is cut that year, and nothing new is bought at all.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 33. a national prize for writing (pairs D6a)
# The writing summit, a facet on [novelist]: a national prize makes a [celebrated author] with [a prize for writing] at
# the real chance (5 to 10): 7 in every colour, the book already on the shortlist. A step at real odds, not a long shot:
# no perk on failure. The gate (LIFE_ART) asks for three years as a novelist (tenure 3). The pairs (all enemy pairs, D6's first half): the next two books signed with the
# old publisher, the tour turned down for the desk, the prize dinner drunk through (giving in), the shortlist money given
# to the library, and a wrong in White and Black: a word asked of a judge.

== a national prize for writing
tier: life event
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-100
holds: novelist
tenure: 3-100
per_year: 0 0 0 .02 .03 .02
drivers: era+.2 fortune+.2
times: per novelist of three years or more a year: about 1 in 50 in mid-life see a book longlisted or shortlisted for a national prize; the big prizes choose one book a year from a shortlist of five or six, and about 1 life in 10,000 becomes a celebrated author (catalogue share; estimate)
likelier: a book that sold and was reviewed, a publisher who enters it, a year when the book's subject is in the air
rarer: a small press that cannot afford the entry, a book out of fashion, a crowded year
gap: 2-5
domain: career.5
tone: joy
life: work, meaning, publiclife
horizon: months
roles: boss, rival, friend, partner, mentor
earth: the latest book is on the shortlist for the prize everyone has heard of
tribal: the elders will honour one maker of verses at the gathering, and every band will learn the verses that win
magic: the academy will give its laurel to one book of the year, and the realm's chronicle will be offered to its writer
scene: The phone rings at breakfast: {Ns} last book is on the shortlist for the prize everyone has heard of, six books, the winner announced at a dinner in the capital in six weeks. By lunch there are interview requests from three papers, and {partner} has opened something fizzy.
scene W: Six weeks of readings, interviews and a dinner in a hired suit. {N} means to do every part of it properly, for the book and for the people in it.
scene U: {N} has read the other five books, and knows exactly how the judges will be comparing them. The question is what {Ns} book does that the others do not.
scene B: A shortlist changes the next contract whatever happens. {N} wants the publisher spending money on this book now, not next year.
scene R: {N} wants to run out into the street. Then, a moment later, wants to hide under the table until it is over.
scene G: The book is about the valley and the people in it, most of them gone now. {N} thinks of them reading about the shortlist in the local paper, and smiles.
scene tribal: At the gathering the elders will honour one maker of verses above the others, and every band will learn the winning verses by heart and carry them home. {Ns} name is spoken at three fires.
scene magic: The academy will give its laurel to one book of the year, and the court has let it be known that the realm's chronicle will be offered to the winner. {Ns} book is one of six still on the masters' table.
- W | go to every reading the prize asks for, and speak about the book with care | title: celebrated author | grants: a prize for writing | v: conformity | tribal: speak the verses at every fire the elders ask, and speak of them with care | magic: go to every reading the academy asks for, and speak about the book with care | chance: 7
- U | show the prize's audiences how the book was built, part by part | title: celebrated author | grants: a prize for writing | v: achievement, conformity | tribal: show the bands how the verses were built, turn by turn | magic: show the academy's audiences how the book was built, part by part | chance: 7
- B | let the publisher spend on the campaign: interviews, posters and parties | title: celebrated author | grants: a prize for writing | identity | v: power, achievement | tribal: let the go-between carry your verses to every fire, and feast the elders who matter | magic: let the printer spend on the campaign: broadsheets, readings and suppers | chance: 7
- R | read the hardest chapter aloud on the shortlist night, and let it land | title: celebrated author | grants: a prize for writing | v: stimulation | tribal: speak the hardest verses at the great fire on the night of choosing, and let them land | magic: read the hardest chapter aloud before the academy, and let it land | chance: 7
- G | take the shortlist tour to the small towns the book is about | title: celebrated author | grants: a prize for writing | aims: celebrated author | v: tradition, benevolence | tribal: carry the verses back to the bands whose stories they tell | magic: take the book to the small towns of the valley it is about | chance: 7
- BG | sign the next two books with the old publisher, at a better advance | grants: a publishing contract | binds | v: power, tradition | tribal: promise your next two tellings to the band's own speakers, for a better share | magic: sign the next two books with the old printer, at a better advance | foot: means money+.06 | chance: 76
- UG | turn the tour down, and go back to the desk for the next book | mark: turned down a chance | self_control: + | v: achievement, security | tribal: stay at your own fire, and go back to making the next telling | magic: turn the readings down, and go back to the desk for the next book | foot: means time+.06 | chance: 76
- UR | drink through the prize dinner, and tell the judges what the prize is worth | mark: made an enemy | self_control: - | v: hedonism, stimulation | tribal: drink the strong brew through the feast, and tell the elders what their honour is worth | magic: drink through the academy's supper, and tell the masters what their laurel is worth | chance: 74
- WB | ask a judge you know from a festival committee to put in a word | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: lobbying a prize judge; backfire: a journalist hears of it, and the publisher withdraws the book from the prize | v: power, security | tribal: ask an elder you know from the gatherings to speak for your verses at the choosing | magic: ask a master you know from the academy's council to put in a word | chance: 74
- WR | give the shortlist money to the library where you first read, and say why | grants: good name in town | v: benevolence, conformity | tribal: give the gifts that came with the shortlist to the old teller who first taught you, and say why | magic: give the purse that came with the shortlist to the free library of your town, and say why | chance: 76
works: The envelope is opened at the dinner, the room turns, and {N} walks up to the stage with the speech folded in a pocket and not needed.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the six weeks pass well, and the book finds more readers than it ever had.
fails: The prize goes to another book, the room applauds, and {N} claps with everyone else and means it, mostly.
fails: The judges' choice is a surprise to everyone, and the shortlist is forgotten by the spring.
